  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Let $I=N$ and ${S}_{i}=[0,\\,1+\\frac{1}{i}],$ then ${\u22c3}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}=$",
  "worked_solution": "The union of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n{\u22c3}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\in U\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{i}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one\u00a0}{A}_{i},\\,\\,i\\in I}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nLet's now write down a few instances of our indexed sets:\n\n- if $i=1,$ we have ${S}_{1}=[0,2]$\n\n- if $i=2,$ we have ${S}_{2}=[0,\\frac{3}{2}]$\n\n- if $i=3,$ we have ${S}_{3}=[0,\\frac{4}{3}]$\n\nand so on. \n\nNotice that as $i$ increases, \n\n- the left endpoint of the intervals is $0$ for all the sets,\n\n- the right endpoint of the intervals approaches $1$ but never reaches it.\n\nMoreover, all the intervals are contained in ${S}_{1}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u22c3}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}={S}_{1}=[0,2].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
